	•	Section 5 Comments/Quotes: The primary endpoint was all-cause mortality within 365 days after transplantation. Table 2 reports AUC 0.87 (95% CI 0.81–0.93), accuracy 0.90 (95% CI 0.85–0.95), sensitivity 0.84, specificity 0.90, PPV 0.82, NPV 0.91, F1-score 0.83, and PR-AUC 0.71 for the Topological Attention model. The abstract separately reports 87.4% accuracy, 84.1% sensitivity, and 89.6% specificity on the test dataset, indicating minor inconsistency or different rounding/evaluation summaries. Calibration was evaluated with curves, Expected Calibration Error 0.08, Brier score 0.12, calibration slope 0.91, and intercept −0.15. DCA showed positive net benefit across thresholds from 0.1 to 0.5 and benefit relative to treat-all and treat-none strategies above approximately 0.15.

Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: The cohort comprised consecutive adult first lung transplant recipients with explicit eligibility criteria, which reduces selective recruitment. However, 24 of 287 screened patients were excluded because more than 20% of key variables were missing and 11 were excluded for incomplete follow-up, without a reported comparison of included and excluded patients; the effect of these exclusions is therefore unclear.
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The high-performing model included postoperative variables such as total ICU length of stay, mechanical-ventilation duration, ECMO duration, acute renal failure, and antibody-mediated rejection “within one year.” Several of these variables occur well after the stated sequential prediction points at transplantation, ICU admission, and ICU day 7, creating substantial temporal leakage and incorporation of future clinical course into mortality prediction.
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The outcome was objectively defined as all-cause mortality within 365 days. Survival status was obtained from hospital records and national death-registry data, and the authors reported no loss to follow-up before the endpoint among included patients.
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Nested cross-validation, fold-specific preprocessing, automated feature selection, class weighting, confidence intervals, calibration, and DCA were important strengths. Nevertheless, only 63 events were available for an initially 76-feature neural-network model, no independent external validation was performed, training accuracy reached 1.00, and postoperative predictors with unclear or outcome-proximal timing create severe optimism and leakage risk. The discrepancy between abstract accuracy and Table 2 also reduces analytical clarity.
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The study directly evaluates an explainable machine-learning model for 1-year mortality after lung transplantation using recipient, operative, and postoperative data. It is directly relevant to AI applications in thoracic organ transplantation, although applicability depends on the clinical time point at which predictions are intended to be made.

